\random returns a random value, and is used as an argument
to other commands, or inside a melodic line. It can also be
called with its shorter alias, \rand.
With one number, it returns an integer from 0 up to the
number minus one:

\random 8

With two numbers, it returns an integer from the first up to
the second minus one:

\random {60 72}

With other values (or with three or more values), it
returns one of them:

\random {c'4 e'4 g'4}

More than one argument must be enclosed in curly brackets.
Inside a bar, the value is chosen once, when the bar is
defined:

\bar rand-1 {
    \time 4/4
    \inst1 c'4 \random {d'4 e'4 f'4} g'2
}

Inside a function, a new value is chosen every time the
function is called:

\function randnote {
    \myosc.send /note \random {60 72}
}

With two arguments, both must be numbers, or both other
values, otherwise it throws an error.
